[[Kotai Jingu (Fujisawa City)]] is a regional [[Sōja (Shinto)|Sōja shrine]]. It enshrines many kami of the local region.<!-- Content taken from [[Sōja (Shinto)]] explaining the concept of it -->

It contrasts with provincial Soja shrines which enshrine all the kami of a province<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/XtZfZ</ref>
[[ja:皇大神宮 (藤沢市)]]
{{統合文字|鵠}}


'''Kōdai-jingū''' (皇大神宮) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社}} located in the {{ill|Kugenuma|ja|鵠沼}} district of {{ill|Fujisawa, Kanagawa|ja|藤沢市}}, Japan. The official name of the shrine is "Kōdai-jingū," but it is also referred to as "Shinmei-gū" or "Karasumori Shrine." It is classified as a {{ill|village shrine|ja|村社}} under the old shrine ranking system. The shrine serves as the chief guardian deity of the former Tōkami district (土甘郷, later Kugenuma Village) and is affectionately known as "Shinmei-sama" (神明様).

== Deities ==
* Main enshrined deities:
** {{ill|Amaterasu|ja|天照大神}} (Amaterasu-Ōmikami)
** [[Ame-no-Koyane]] (Amenokoyane-no-Mikoto)
** [[Ame-no-Tajikarao]] (Amenotajikarao-no-Mikoto)
** {{ill|Futodama|ja|天太玉命}} (Amenofutodama-no-Mikoto)
** {{ill|Ame-no-Uzume|ja|天宇受売命}} (Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto)
** Ishikoridome-no-Mikoto (石凝刀売命)
* Additional enshrined deities:
** {{ill|Ihatate-no-Ōkami|ja|石楯尾大神}} (Ihatate-Ōkami)
** {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡神}} (Hachiman-Ōkami)
** {{ill|Kasuga-taisha|ja|春日神}} (Kasuga-Ōkami)

== History ==
[[File:Kugenuma Kōdai-jingū -Torii.jpg|thumb|250px|First torii, reconstructed in 1960 with reinforced concrete.]]
* {{ill|Daidō (era)|ja|大同}} 3 ({{ill|808|ja}}): A {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社}} known as "Ihatate-no-Jinja" was established on the current site of Shinmei-gū.
* {{ill|Tenchō (Japanese era)|ja|天長}} 9 ({{ill|832|ja}}): The shrine building for Karasumori Shrine (Kōdai-jingū) was constructed on the same grounds as Ihatate Shrine. This is considered the founding year of the shrine.
* {{ill|Ten'an (Japanese era)|ja|天安}} 1 ({{ill|857|ja}}): Ihatate Shrine was ranked as a "Kansha" (officially recognized shrine) at the rank of "Jūgoi."
* During the reign of Emperor Daigo (醍醐天皇): The shrine was designated as the chief shrine of Tōkami district, known as "Shinmei-gū."
* {{ill|Tenki|ja|天喜}} era (1046–1057): {{ill|Minamoto no Yoshiie|ja|源義家}} (Hachiman Tarō Yoshiie) offered prayers at the shrine during his campaign to subdue the Ōshū region.
* {{ill|Eikyū (Heian period)|ja|永久}} 5 (1117): The shrine was designated as the chief guardian shrine of the Ōba Manor (大庭御厨) area and renamed "Kōdai-jingū."
* {{ill|Eiroku (Japanese era)|ja|永禄}} era: The shrine continued to serve as a key religious site for the area, undergoing multiple reconstructions and expansions.

== Buildings and Grounds ==
[[File:Kugenuma Kōdai-jingū -Ise-no-miya.jpg|thumb|250px|<center>Ise-no-Miya Shrine within the grounds.</center>]]
The shrine grounds cover an area of 3,300 tsubo and feature old-growth trees, including hundreds-of-years-old tabunoki (machilus) and zelkova, forming a sacred forest. The shrine's name "Karasumori" (Crow Forest) originates from the large number of crows inhabiting the area.
* Main shrine: Constructed in the {{ill|Shinmei-zukuri|ja|神明造}} style, the current structure was rebuilt in 1984 after nearly 400 years.
* Subsidiary shrines:
  ** {{ill|Ontake Shrine|ja|御嶽神社}}
  ** {{ill|Akiba Shrine|ja|秋葉神社}}
  ** {{ill|Ihatate-no-Jinja|ja|石楯尾神社}}
  ** Ise-no-Miya
  ** {{ill|Inari Shrine|ja|稲荷神社}}
  ** Other small shrines dedicated to regional and national deities.
* Festival floats storage: Dedicated spaces for 9 traditional floats used during the shrine's annual festival.

== Festivals and Annual Events ==
[[File:勢揃い１.jpg|thumb|250px|<center>Festival floats at the annual festival.</center>]]
* New Year’s Festival (January 1)
* festival (August 17)
  ** Known as one of the major festivals in Kanagawa Prefecture, featuring 9 ornate floats and traditional performances.
* Monthly offerings (1st and 15th of each month)
* Seasonal rites, including rituals for good harvest, protection from insects, and exorcisms.

== Access ==
The shrine is located at 2-11-5 Kugenuma Shinmei, Fujisawa, Kanagawa.
* Nearest stations: {{ill|JR Fujisawa Station|ja|藤沢駅}} or nearby bus stops.
* Parking available.

