[[Kōdai-jingū]] is a regional [[Sōja shrine|Sōja shrine]]. It enshrines many kami of the local region.

It contrasts with provincial Soja shrines which enshrine all the kami of a province<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/XtZfZ</ref>

{{統合文字|鵠}}

'''Kōdai-jingū''' (皇大神宮) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|qid=Q845945|lt=Shinto shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} located in the {{ill|Kugenuma|ja|鵠沼|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kugenuma|1=Kugenuma|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q11675779}} district of {{ill|Fujisawa, Kanagawa|en|Fujisawa, Kanagawa|qid=Q386661|lt=Fujisawa, Kanagawa|1=Fujisawa, Kanagawa}}, Japan. The official name of the shrine is "Kōdai-jingū," but it is also referred to as "Shinmei-gū" or "Karasumori Shrine." It is classified as a {{ill|village shrine|ja|村社|1=Son-sha|qid=Q134917284}}{{efn|Former shrine rank|lt=Son-sha|1=modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/village shrine|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q712534}} under the old shrine Ranking system. The shrine serves as the chief guardian deity of the former Tōkami district (土甘郷, later Kugenuma Village) and is affectionately known as "Shinmei-sama" (神明様).

== Deities ==
* Main enshrined deities:
** {{ill|Amaterasu|en|Amaterasu|qid=Q455602|lt=Amaterasu Ōmikami|1=Amaterasu}} (Amaterasu-Ōmikami)
** [[Ame-no-Koyane]] (Amenokoyane-no-Mikoto)
** {{ill|Ame-no-Tajikarao|en|Ame-no-Tajikarao|qid=Q849412|lt=Ame-no-Tajikarao|1=Ame-no-Tajikarao}} (Amenotajikarao-no-Mikoto)
** {{ill|Futodama|ja|天太玉命|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Futodama|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to フトダマ}} (Amenofutodama-no-Mikoto)
** {{ill|Ame-no-Uzume|ja|天宇受売命|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Uzume|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to アメノウズメ}} (Ame-no-Uzume-no-Mikoto)
** Ishikoridome-no-Mikoto (石凝刀売命)
* Additional enshrined deities:
** {{ill|Ihatate-no-Ōkami|ja|石楯尾大神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ihatate-no-Ōkami|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} (Ihatate-Ōkami)
** {{ill|Hachiman|en|Hachiman|qid=Q261637|lt=Hachiman|1=Hachiman}} (Hachiman-Ōkami)
** {{ill|Kasuga-taisha|ja|春日神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kasuga-taisha|qq=}} (Kasuga-Ōkami)

== History ==
[[File:Kugenuma Kōdai-jingū -Torii.jpg|thumb|250px|First torii, reconstructed in 1960 with reinforced concrete.]]
* {{ill|Datong (disambiguation)|en|Datong (disambiguation)|qid=Q295082|lt=Daidō (era)|1=Datong (disambiguation)}} 3 ({{ill|808|en|808|qid=Q399792|lt=808|1=808}}): A {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|qid=Q845945|lt=Shinto shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} known as "Ihatate-no-Jinja" was established on the current site of Shinmei-gū.
* {{ill|Tenchō|en|Tenchō|qid=Q1373138|lt=Tenchō (Japanese era)|1=Tenchō}} 9 ({{ill|832|en|832|qid=Q23900|lt=832|1=832}}): The shrine building for Karasumori Shrine (Kōdai-jingū) was constructed on the same grounds as Ihatate Shrine. This is considered the founding year of the shrine.
* {{ill|Ten'an (Japanese era)|ja|天安|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ten'an (Japanese era)|1=Cheonan|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q3577288}} 1 ({{ill|857|en|857|qid=Q30374|lt=857|1=857}}): Ihatate Shrine was Ranked as a "Kansha" (officially recognized shrine) at the Rank of "Jūgoi."
* During the reign of Emperor Daigo (醍醐天皇): The shrine was designated as the chief shrine of Tōkami district, known as "Shinmei-gū."
* {{ill|Tengi|en|Tengi|qid=Q1193591|lt=Tenki|1=Tengi}} era (1046–1057): {{ill|Minamoto no Yoshiie|en|Minamoto no Yoshiie|qid=Q32613|lt=Minamoto no Yoshiie|1=Minamoto no Yoshiie}} (Hachiman Tarō Yoshiie) offered prayers at the shrine during his campaign to subdue the Ōshū region.
* {{ill|Eikyū (Heian period)|ja|永久|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Eikyū (Heian period)|1=Eikyū (Japan)|comment=changed title based on wikidata|qid=Q16277931}} 5 (1117): The shrine was designated as the chief guardian shrine of the Ōba Manor (大庭御厨) area and renamed "Kōdai-jingū."
* {{ill|Eiroku|en|Eiroku|qid=Q1143341|lt=Eiroku (Japanese era)|1=Eiroku}} era: The shrine continued to serve as a key religious site for the area, undergoing multiple reconstructions and expansions.

== Buildings and Grounds ==
[[File:Kugenuma Kōdai-jingū -Ise-no-miya.jpg|thumb|250px|<center>Ise-no-Miya Shrine within the grounds.</center>]]
The shrine grounds cover an area of 3,300 tsubo and feature old-growth trees, including hundreds-of-years-old tabunoki (machilus) and zelkova, forming a sacred forest. The shrine's name "Karasumori" (Crow Forest) originates from the large number of crows inhabiting the area.
* Main shrine: Constructed in the {{ill|Shinmei-zukuri|en|Shinmei-zukuri|qid=Q930545|lt=Shinmei-zukuri|1=Shinmei-zukuri}} style, the current structure was rebuilt in 1984 after nearly 400 years.
